.TH PEEKASTOKK 1 "2026-07-18" "PeekAStokk" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
peekastokk \- live log viewer in the browser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B peekastokk
[\fIflags\fR] [\fIlogfile\fR|\fIdirectory\fR|\fIglob\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B peekastokk
tails one or more log files and streams them live to an embedded web UI
over Server-Sent Events. Point it at plain files, directories (every file
directly inside one is tailed), or glob patterns
.RI ( /var/log/*.log ),
then open the printed URL in a browser to filter, search, and follow the
files as they grow.
.PP
It survives log rotation (both rename+recreate and copytruncate styles),
waits for files that do not exist yet, and bounds its own memory use
regardless of how large or fast-moving the tailed files are. Nothing is
persisted to disk by the program itself; the log files it reads are the
only state.
.SH ARGUMENTS
Each positional argument is one of:
.TP
.I logfile
A plain path. If it does not exist yet,
.B peekastokk
waits for it to be created.
.TP
.I directory
Every regular file directly inside the directory is tailed as its own
log. Dot-files and subdirectories are skipped (not recursive). The
directory is re-scanned every
.B \-rescan
so files created later are picked up and deleted ones stop being tailed;
with re-scanning disabled, an empty directory is a startup error.
.TP
.I glob
A shell glob pattern such as
.I '/var/log/*.log'
or
.IR '/var/log/myproject*' .
Quote it so your shell passes it through unexpanded; matching directories
are skipped. The pattern is re-scanned every
.B \-rescan
like a directory; with re-scanning disabled, a pattern that matches
nothing is a startup error.
.PP
Any argument may carry an
.BI : alias
suffix that sets the display name of what it expands to. On a
.I logfile
the alias is the name outright; on a
.I directory
or
.I glob
\(em which expand to many files \(em it prefixes each file's own base
name (\fI/var/log/myapp/:worker1\fR becomes
.IR worker1/app.log ", " worker1/error.log ", ...),"
so same-named logs from different places stay apart. The alias may
contain letters, digits, and
.BR . ", " _ " and " \- ;
only the last colon splits, so a path containing a colon still works
(give it an explicit alias if its last segment looks like one). Aliases
also name the source when forwarding.
.PP
If no arguments are given, the files listed as
.B file
entries in the configuration file are used instead (see
.B CONFIGURATION FILE
below). At least one file, directory, or pattern is required from one
source or the other.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-addr " address"
HTTP listen address. Default:
.IR 127.0.0.1:8844 .
Binding to anything other than loopback should be paired with
.B \-auth
and a TLS-terminating reverse proxy; see
.B SECURITY
below.
.TP
.BI \-history " n"
Number of recent lines the server keeps in memory and replays to a newly
connected or reconnecting browser. Default:
.IR 2000 .
.TP
.BI \-lines " n"
Default number of lines the web UI keeps rendered on screen; adjustable
live in the UI, where a browser's own choice is then remembered across
reloads. Default:
.IR 500 .
.TP
.BI \-poll " duration"
How often each tailed file is checked for new data. Default:
.IR 200ms .
.TP
.BI \-rescan " duration"
How often directory and glob arguments are re-scanned for created or
deleted log files. A deleted file's tailer stops and its entry leaves
the file picker; the same file reappearing (log rotation, redeploys)
revives it.
.I 0
disables re-scanning: arguments expand once at startup. Default:
.IR 60s .
.TP
.BI \-tail\-bytes " n"
Maximum bytes of a file's existing content replayed at startup, aligned
to the next line boundary. A negative value starts at the end of the
file instead (no replay). Default:
.IR 65536 .
.TP
.BI \-max\-line\-bytes " n"
Lines longer than this are split into chunks, so a file without newlines
cannot grow memory without bound. Default:
.IR 262144 .
.TP
.BI \-log\-level " level"
One of
.IR debug ", " info ", " warn ", or " error .
Default:
.IR info .
.TP
.BI \-auth " user:password"
Require HTTP basic authentication to view the UI, the event stream, and
the API; empty (the default) leaves it open. The password half may be a
plaintext string or an Argon2id hash produced by
.B \-hash\-password
(preferred \(em see
.B SECURITY
below).
Passing
.B \-auth
with an empty value on the command line disables authentication even
when the configuration file enables it.
.TP
.B \-hash\-password
Prompt for a password (input hidden, asked twice, on a terminal; read as
a single piped line otherwise), print its Argon2id hash in PHC string
format, and exit. Paste the result after
.IR user: " in the " auth " setting."
.TP
.BI \-config " path"
Load configuration from this file instead of searching the standard
locations (see
.B FILES
below).
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-headless
Do not serve the web UI; tail and forward only. A pure forwarding client
sets this together with
.BR \-forward\-to .
.TP
.BI \-forward\-to " url"
Push every tailed line to another PeekAStokk server's /ingest endpoint
(http(s)://host:port). Requires
.BR \-forward\-token .
While the connection is down, up to
.B \-forward\-buffer\-lines
lines (default 5000) are buffered in memory and redelivered on
reconnect; nothing is written to disk.
.TP
.BI \-forward\-token " token"
Bearer token authenticating this client to the receiving server.
Generate with
.B \-generate\-token
on the server side; the server maps the token to a client name via its
.I ingest
configuration entries, and that name prefixes every forwarded source's
display name.
.TP
.BI \-status\-addr " address"
Serve a minimal monitoring listener (/healthz, /statusz) on this
address, independent of
.BR \-headless .
Exposes connection state and counters, never log content.
.TP
.B \-docker
Tail Docker container logs found under
.BR \-docker\-root ,
read-only, with no Docker socket access. Container json-log lines are
unwrapped automatically and container starts/stops are tracked by
polling.
.TP
.BI \-docker\-root " dir"
Docker's containers directory. Default
.IR /var/lib/docker/containers ;
if the daemon's data-root was moved (custom \-\-data\-root, rootless
Docker), use the output of
.I docker info \-\-format '{{.DockerRootDir}}'
plus
.IR /containers .
.TP
.BI \-docker\-containers " entry"
Repeatable container selection: an exact name or id (optionally
.IR name:alias ,
displaying the container as
.IR alias ),
a glob like
.IR 'web\-*' ,
or
.I *
for all containers (the default when no entries are given). An alias is
only valid on an exact entry.
.TP
.BI \-docker\-poll " duration"
How often the containers directory is re-scanned. Default:
.IR 2s .
.TP
.B \-generate\-token
Generate a high-entropy forwarding token: print the plaintext once (for
the client's
.BR \-forward\-token )
and its Argon2id hash (for the server's
.I ingest
entry), then exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Every setting
.B peekastokk
accepts as a flag, plus a repeatable
.B file
entry for the tailed files (each taking the same optional
.BI : alias
suffix as a command-line argument), may instead live in a plain
.IR "key = value"
configuration file. Command-line flags override the configuration file,
which overrides built-in defaults; log files given as command-line
arguments replace the configuration file's
.B file
list entirely rather than merging with it.
.PP
Recognized keys:
.IR addr ", " port " (shorthand for the port half of " addr "), " history ,
.IR lines ", " poll ", " rescan ", " tail\-bytes ", " max\-line\-bytes ", " log\-level ,
.IR auth ", and the repeatable " file .
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
or
.B ;
are ignored, as is an unquoted trailing comment (a
.B #
preceded by whitespace). Quote a value to keep a literal
.B #
or surrounding spaces. In
.B file
values a leading
.B ~
expands to the home directory and relative paths resolve against the
directory containing the configuration file itself. An unknown or
malformed key is a startup error naming the offending line.
.PP
Example:
.nf
.RS
port      = 9000
history   = 5000
log\-level = info

file = /var/log/app.log
file = ~/logs/worker.log
file = /var/log/nginx/*.log
.RE
.fi
.PP
A fully commented, ready-to-copy example ships as
.I config.example
in the project repository, and is installed to
.I /etc/peekastokk/config.example
by
.I install.sh
(see
.B FILES
below).
.SH ENDPOINTS
The web UI is a single embedded page served at
.IR / .
It also exposes:
.TP
.I /events
The Server-Sent Events stream (JSON lines). Accepts a repeatable
.I files
query parameter (opaque per-file ids, not paths) to limit the stream to
selected files, and an
.I after
parameter to resume past a sequence number.
.TP
.I /api/files
The list of tailed files as opaque ids and display names; absolute paths
are never exposed to the client.
.TP
.I /api/before
Older lines read backwards from disk for the UI's scrollback, bounded to
files this process is actually tailing.
.TP
.I /healthz
A liveness probe. Always reachable without authentication, even when
.B \-auth
is set.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Overrides the default location searched for a user-level configuration
file (see
.B FILES
below). Not required; only consulted when
.B \-config
is not given.
.SH FILES
Unless
.B \-config
is given, a configuration file is searched for in this order, and the
first one found is used:
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/peekastokk/config
Defaults to
.I ~/.config/peekastokk/config
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.I ~/.peekastokk
A plain file.
.TP
.I ~/.peekastokk/config
.TP
.I /etc/peekastokk/config
An unconditional system-wide fallback, checked last after every
user-specific location. This is what a service account with no
meaningful home directory resolves to, with no extra environment setup
required \(em see the
.B "Running as a service"
section of the project README for running it this way.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on a clean shutdown (SIGINT or SIGTERM);
.B 1
on any startup failure, including a port already in use, a malformed
configuration file, or no log files resolved from arguments or
configuration.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
trigger a graceful shutdown: the HTTP server stops accepting new
connections, in-flight Server-Sent Events streams are closed, and file
tailers are stopped before the process exits. This is what
.I systemctl stop
sends.
.SH SECURITY
.B peekastokk
serves whatever it tails, without authentication, unless
.B \-auth
is set. It binds to loopback by default; if exposed on another interface
with
.BR \-addr ,
enable
.B \-auth
and put a TLS-terminating reverse proxy in front, since HTTP basic
authentication is sent unencrypted otherwise. Prefer an Argon2id hash
(via
.BR \-hash\-password )
over a plaintext password in the configuration file, which is only ever
readable by root and the service group when installed via the project's
.I install.sh
(mode 0640).
.PP
Absolute file paths never reach the client: the API, the event stream,
and the scrollback endpoint all speak in opaque per-file ids and
deduplicated base names, never the underlying path.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Tail two specific files:
.B "peekastokk /var/log/app.log /var/log/nginx/access.log"
.TP
Tail every file in a directory:
.B "peekastokk /var/log/myapp/"
.TP
Tail by glob pattern (quoted so the shell does not expand it):
.B "peekastokk '/var/log/*.log'"
.TP
Listen on all interfaces with authentication:
.B "peekastokk \-addr 0.0.0.0:8844 \-auth dev:s3cret /var/log/app.log"
.TP
Generate a password hash for the configuration file:
.B "peekastokk \-hash\-password"
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation, the annotated
.IR config.example ,
and the systemd service instructions:
.RI < https://github.com/shellsecrets/peekastokk >
